window.COURSE.notes = window.COURSE.notes || {};
window.COURSE.notes["ls"] = [{"n": 1, "title": "Foundations of English for Engineers", "tagline": "Language Skills for Engineers · module 1", "minutes": 12, "sections": [{"kind": "concept", "num": "1", "title": "SESSION 1 — Introduction & Why Language Accuracy Matters", "html": "<h4>Why Accuracy Matters</h4>\n<p><strong>Key Formula (important for short-answer):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Technical Expertise + Communication Discipline (Clarity + Structure + Audience Focus) = Engineering Impact (Faster Decisions + Trust + Lower Risk)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Accurate communication converts expertise into impact — the value of engineering work depends on how clearly results are shared.</li>\n<li><strong>Clarity</strong> → avoids misunderstanding, shows audience consideration, builds trust, demonstrates respect, leaves a lasting impression.</li>\n<li><strong>Credibility</strong> → built by citing sources, showing data, open body language, speaking clearly to audience needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Expanded reasons (from detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reason</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Credibility</strong></td>\n<td>Errors in writing make readers doubt your technical competence, even if your work is excellent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Safety</strong></td>\n<td>In engineering documentation (manuals, specifications, safety instructions), an unclear sentence can lead to real-world accidents.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Efficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Ambiguous writing forces colleagues to ask for clarification, wasting time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Global Collaboration</strong></td>\n<td>Standard, accurate English ensures multinational teams understand the same thing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Career Growth</strong></td>\n<td>Reports, emails, resumes, and presentations are judged partly on language quality.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Language is the <em>packaging</em> for your technical ideas — even the best product (idea) can fail to sell if the packaging (language) is torn or confusing.</p>\n<h4>5-Minute Daily Improvement Routines <em>(list-type question likely)</em></h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Read emails aloud before sending</li>\n<li>\"Draft Delay\" — wait 2 minutes before sending</li>\n<li>Cut unnecessary words from drafts</li>\n<li>Read industry articles 10 min/day</li>\n<li>Write 3 clear sentences daily</li>\n<li>Swap weak words for precise terms</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Grammar Focus: Subject–Verb Agreement</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Incorrect</th>\n<th>Correct</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>They goes to the gym.</td>\n<td>They go to the gym.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The paper or the ruler are in the desk.</td>\n<td>The paper or the ruler <strong>is</strong> in the desk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The data <em>is</em> incomplete and the results <em>is</em> also wrong.</td>\n<td>The data <em>are</em> incomplete and the results <em>are</em> also wrong. <em>(note: \"data is\" is now widely accepted informally in tech usage — check institution's preferred style)</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> Subject and verb must agree in number (singular/singular, plural/plural). With \"or\"/\"nor,\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>[Extra — not tied to a session] Sentence Fundamentals</h4>\n<p>A sentence expresses a <strong>complete thought</strong> and needs a <strong>Subject</strong> (who/what) + <strong>Verb/Predicate</strong> (what it does or is).\nExample: <em>\"The engineer tests the circuit.\"</em> → Subject = <em>the engineer</em>, Verb = <em>tests</em>, Object = <em>the circuit</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Types by structure:</strong>\n| Type | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Simple | One independent clause | \"The server crashed.\" |\n| Compound | Two independent clauses + conjunction (and, but, or, so) | \"The server crashed, but the backup worked.\" |\n| Complex | Independent clause + dependent clause | \"Because the code had a bug, the server crashed.\" |\n| Compound-Complex | Combination of both | \"Because the code had a bug, the server crashed, but the backup saved the data.\" |</p>\n<p><strong>Types by purpose:</strong> Declarative (states fact), Interrogative (asks question), Imperative (gives command), Exclamatory (expresses emotion).</p>\n<p><strong>Building blocks:</strong> Clause (subject+verb, independent or dependent), Phrase (no subject-verb pair), Independent Clause (stands alone), Dependent/Subordinate Clause (cannot stand alone).</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2", "title": "SESSION 2 — Accuracy, Clarity & Register", "html": "<p><strong>Aim:</strong> Correct errors caused by mother-tongue influence, build self-editing skills, use AI as a learning tool.</p>\n<h4>Translation Triads</h4>\n<p>Think in mother tongue, but <em>translate the meaning</em>, not word-for-word.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mother Tongue</th>\n<th>Direct (wrong)</th>\n<th>Correct English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Naku thala noppiga undhi</td>\n<td>To me headache/pain is there</td>\n<td>I have a headache</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Run-ons vs. Fragments</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Run-on sentence:</strong> two complete sentences wrongly joined.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>I finished my homework I went to bed.</em></li>\n<li>✅ <em>I finished my homework, and I went to bed.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Sentence fragment:</strong> incomplete thought, missing subject/verb/completeness.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>Because I was tired.</em></li>\n<li>✅ <em>Because I was tired, I went to bed early.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Dangling Modifiers</h4>\n<p>Phrase doesn't clearly modify the intended subject (subject is missing/misplaced).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ <em>Walking to school, the rain started to fall.</em> (implies rain was walking)</li>\n<li>✅ <em>As I was walking to school, the rain started to fall.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>[Extra — not tied to a session] Common Error Patterns of Indian/Engineering English</h4>\n<p>Recurring habits from regional languages or casual spoken English — awareness is the first step to correction.</p>\n<p><strong>Article Errors (a, an, the):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"He is engineer working in software company.\" → ✅ \"He is <em>an</em> engineer working in <em>a</em> software company.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Preposition Errors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"Discuss about the problem.\" → ✅ \"Discuss the problem.\" (discuss is never followed by \"about\")</li>\n<li>❌ \"He is good in mathematics.\" → ✅ \"He is good <em>at</em> mathematics.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Redundancy / Repetition (Pleonasm):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"Revert back to me\" → ✅ \"Revert to me\" / \"Reply to me\"</li>\n<li>❌ \"Repeat again\" → ✅ \"Repeat\"</li>\n<li>❌ \"Free gift\" → ✅ \"Gift\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Direct Translation from Native Language:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"I am having a doubt.\" → ✅ \"I have a doubt\" / \"I'm not sure about this.\"</li>\n<li>❌ \"What is your good name?\" → ✅ \"What is your name?\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tense Confusion:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"I am completing the project since Monday.\" → ✅ \"I have been completing the project since Monday.\" / \"I completed the project on Monday.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Overuse of Passive Voice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"It is being informed that the meeting is postponed by the committee.\" → ✅ \"The committee postponed the meeting.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wordy/Formal-Sounding but Incorrect Phrasing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"Kindly do the needful.\" → ✅ \"Please complete this task.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Using AI as an Accuracy Tutor</h4>\n<p>Ask AI to <em>explain</em>, not just fix:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Correct only the mistakes. Explain why each correction is needed. Tell me which grammar rule applies.\"</li>\n<li>\"Analyze my mistakes. Tell me whether they are Grammar, Vocabulary, Tense, etc.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Expanded guidance (detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Grammar checking with explanations</strong> — ask AI to explain <em>why</em>, e.g. <em>\"Correct this sentence and explain the grammar rule: 'He don't like testing.'\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Rewriting for register/tone</strong> — ask AI to rewrite the same content formally vs. informally to see the difference.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice with common error patterns</strong> — ask AI to generate practice sentences on your weak areas (articles, prepositions).</li>\n<li><strong>Proofreading assistance, not replacement</strong> — try to spot errors yourself first, use AI to confirm.</li>\n<li><strong>Simplifying complex text</strong> — ask AI to simplify a passage to check your own understanding.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Cautions:</strong> Don't blindly trust AI output — cross-check important documents yourself or with a human reviewer. Understand the \"why,\" not just the \"what.\" AI can occasionally apply an overly formal/informal register — review context before using suggestions.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3", "title": "SESSION 3 — Clarity Principles, Tone, Concision & Varieties", "html": "<h4>Four Core Clarity Principles <em>(very likely exam list — memorize order)</em></h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>One Idea Per Sentence</strong> — avoid long, complex sentences.</li>\n<li><strong>Active vs. Passive Voice</strong></li>\n<li>Active → use when the <em>doer</em> matters (team updates, achievements). E.g., <em>The team submitted the report.</em></li>\n<li>Passive → use when the <em>action/result</em> matters (lab reports, technical docs). E.g., <em>The report was submitted.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Parallel Structure</strong> — keep list items in the same grammatical form.</li>\n<li>❌ <em>swimming, to run, cycling</em> → ✅ <em>swimming, running, cycling</em></li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Ambiguity</strong> — use specific, measurable terms so there's only one interpretation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Additional clarity principles (detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <strong>concrete, specific words</strong>: ❌ \"There was an issue with the system.\" → ✅ \"The server crashed due to a memory overflow.\"</li>\n<li>Keep sentence length moderate (<strong>15–20 words</strong> average for technical writing).</li>\n<li>Avoid unnecessary jargon for a general audience.</li>\n<li><strong>Place modifiers close to what they describe:</strong></li>\n<li>❌ \"She almost drove her car for six hours.\" (implies she almost drove, but didn't)</li>\n<li>✅ \"She drove her car for almost six hours.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Register and Tone</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Register</th>\n<th>Style</th>\n<th>Audience</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal</td>\n<td>Structured, objective, respectful, concise, solution-oriented</td>\n<td>Manager/Principal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neutral</td>\n<td>Clear, direct, matter-of-fact, collaborative, friendly</td>\n<td>Colleague/Peer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Informal</td>\n<td>Casual, conversational</td>\n<td>Friend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Expanded guidance for engineers (detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Technical reports &amp; documentation:</strong> Always formal, objective, third-person where possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Emails to seniors/clients:</strong> Formal-to-neutral, polite, precise.</li>\n<li><strong>Team chat/Slack messages:</strong> Neutral to informal, but still professional (no slang with clients).</li>\n<li>Avoid mixing registers within the same document — looks inconsistent and unprofessional.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tone</strong> reflects the writer's attitude (respectful, urgent, apologetic, confident) — even factual writing carries a tone through word choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"The system <em>failed catastrophically</em>\" (dramatic tone) vs. \"The system <em>did not meet expected performance</em>\" (neutral, professional tone).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Concision — 4 Techniques</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace wordy phrases → <em>due to the fact that</em> → <em>because</em></li>\n<li>Eliminate redundancies → <em>end result</em> → <em>result</em></li>\n<li>Remove filler words → <em>basically, actually</em></li>\n<li>Cut prepositional strings</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Extra concision examples (detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Wordy</th>\n<th>Concise</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"at this point in time\"</td>\n<td>\"now\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"in order to\"</td>\n<td>\"to\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"a large number of\"</td>\n<td>\"many\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"make a decision\"</td>\n<td>\"decide\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid unnecessary qualifiers: ❌ \"It is very extremely important to test the code.\" → ✅ \"It is important to test the code.\"</li>\n<li>Use strong verbs instead of noun phrases: ❌ \"We conducted an investigation of the failure.\" → ✅ \"We investigated the failure.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Standard Varieties &amp; Global Intelligibility</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use simple, standard vocabulary (<em>solve this problem</em>, not <em>figure this out</em>)</li>\n<li>Pick <strong>one</strong> spelling variant (UK or US) and stay consistent</li>\n<li>Write in <strong>Plain English</strong>; avoid regional idioms (<em>\"do the needful,\" \"prepone\"</em>)</li>\n<li>Format dates universally: <strong>3 May 2026</strong> (not 3/5/26, which is ambiguous)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Expanded (detailed notes):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consistency matters more than \"correctness\" of one variety over another</strong> — don't mix British (\"colour\") and American (\"color\") spelling in one document.</li>\n<li><strong>Intelligibility</strong> = how easily your English is understood by a global, non-native-speaking audience.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Indian English</th>\n<th>International Standard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Prepone the meeting\"</td>\n<td>\"Move the meeting earlier\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Out of station\"</td>\n<td>\"Out of town\" / \"Unavailable\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Pass out (graduate)\"</td>\n<td>\"Graduate\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Do the needful\"</td>\n<td>\"Take the necessary action\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4", "title": "SESSION 4 — Punctuation for Clarity", "html": "<p><strong>Classic example (meaning changes with punctuation):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Let's eat teachers.</em> vs. <em>Let's eat, teachers.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Key Punctuation Marks</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mark</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full Stop / Period (.)</td>\n<td>Ends a declarative sentence</td>\n<td>\"The test passed.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comma (,)</td>\n<td>Separates clauses/ideas/list items; signals a pause</td>\n<td>She wanted to go for a walk, but it started raining.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Semicolon (;)</td>\n<td>Links two closely related complete sentences, no conjunction</td>\n<td>The weather was terrible; nevertheless, we went hiking.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colon (:)</td>\n<td>Introduces a list, explanation, example, or quote after a complete statement</td>\n<td>There was only one problem: we were completely lost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apostrophe (')</td>\n<td>Shows possession or forms contractions</td>\n<td>Sarah's book, don't</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Additional marks (detailed notes):</strong>\n| Mark | Purpose | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quotation Marks (\" \") | Indicates exact words or a quoted term | The function is called <code>\"main()\"</code>. |\n| Hyphen (-) | Joins compound words | \"Real-time system,\" \"well-designed code\" |\n| Parentheses ( ) | Adds extra, non-essential information | \"The algorithm (developed in 2020) is still in use.\" |</p>\n<h4>Common Punctuation Mistakes (detailed notes)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comma splice</strong> — joining two full sentences with only a comma:</li>\n<li>❌ \"The test failed, we need to debug it.\" → ✅ \"The test failed; we need to debug it.\" / \"The test failed, so we need to debug it.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Missing comma after introductory phrase:</strong></li>\n<li>❌ \"After the update the system crashed.\" → ✅ \"After the update, the system crashed.\"</li>\n<li><strong>its vs. it's:</strong> <em>its</em> = possessive (\"The robot moved its arm.\"); <em>it's</em> = \"it is\" (\"It's working correctly.\")</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clarity Check (concluding point):</strong> Proper punctuation + appropriate register → clear, meaningful writing → prevents misunderstanding → improves readability → builds strong professional relationships.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5", "title": "[Extra — not tied to a session] Proofreading and Self-Editing Fundamentals", "html": "<p><strong>Why proofread:</strong> Even skilled writers make errors; proofreading catches mistakes before your reader does.</p>\n<p><strong>The Proofreading Process:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take a break before proofreading — fresh eyes catch more errors.</li>\n<li>Read aloud — awkward sentences and missing words become obvious when spoken.</li>\n<li>Check one type of error at a time:</li>\n<li>Pass 1: Grammar and sentence structure</li>\n<li>Pass 2: Punctuation</li>\n<li>Pass 3: Spelling and word choice</li>\n<li>Pass 4: Formatting and consistency</li>\n<li>Read backwards (sentence by sentence) for spelling checks.</li>\n<li>Check for consistency in terms, abbreviations, and formatting.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Self-Editing Checklist:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>[ ] Does every sentence have a clear subject and verb?</li>\n<li>[ ] Are subject and verb in agreement (singular/plural)?</li>\n<li>[ ] Are articles (a/an/the) used correctly?</li>\n<li>[ ] Is punctuation used correctly (no comma splices, correct apostrophes)?</li>\n<li>[ ] Is the tone/register appropriate for the audience?</li>\n<li>[ ] Have redundant words/phrases been removed?</li>\n<li>[ ] Is passive voice used only when necessary?</li>\n<li>[ ] Are technical terms used consistently?</li>\n</ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick Revision Summary (session-tagged, last-minute recall)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S1:</strong> Accuracy = Impact. Formula: Expertise + Communication Discipline = Engineering Impact. Subject–verb agreement rule. <em>(+ Extra: sentence fundamentals — subject/verb, sentence types.)</em></li>\n<li><strong>S2:</strong> Translate meaning, not words. Fix run-ons/fragments/dangling modifiers. Use AI to explain, not just correct. <em>(+ Extra: article/preposition/redundancy/tense/passive-voice error patterns.)</em></li>\n<li><strong>S3:</strong> 4 Clarity Principles (One Idea/Sentence, Active vs Passive, Parallel Structure, Avoid Ambiguity) + Register (Formal/Neutral/Informal) + Concision (4 techniques) + Global English varieties.</li>\n<li><strong>S4:</strong> 5+ punctuation marks and their functions; punctuation changes meaning; comma splices and intro-phrase commas.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra (no session):</strong> Sentence fundamentals, common Indian/engineering English error patterns, proofreading &amp; self-editing process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Likely exam angles:</strong> definitions + one example each; correct-the-error questions (run-ons, fragments, dangling modifiers, subject-verb agreement, articles, prepositions); register identification for a given scenario; punctuation insertion/correction; short answer on \"why clarity matters.\"</p>"}], "practice": [], "file": "Semester_1/Language-Skills-Notes/Module1-Notes.md"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Vocabulary and Word Precision", "tagline": "Language Skills for Engineers · module 2", "minutes": 13, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Overview", "html": "<h3>Merged Notes (Session-wise notes + Detailed notes combined by topic)</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Each heading is tagged with the <strong>Session</strong> it belongs to from the session-wise notes. Content that only appears in the detailed notes is folded into the session it expands on, and clearly marked as an expansion.</p>\n</blockquote>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1", "title": "SESSION 5 — How Professional Vocabulary is Built", "html": "<p><strong>Aim:</strong> Understand how words are formed (roots/prefixes/suffixes) to decode unfamiliar technical vocabulary and build comprehension.</p>\n<h4>Word Parts <em>(very likely fill-in/match question)</em></h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Root</td>\n<td>Core meaning of a word</td>\n<td><em>bio</em> = life, <em>geo</em> = earth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prefix</td>\n<td>Added <strong>before</strong> a word</td>\n<td><em>re-</em> = again, <em>inter-</em> = between</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suffix</td>\n<td>Added <strong>after</strong> a word</td>\n<td><em>-tion</em> = process, <em>-ment</em> = result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Worked Examples</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Telecommunication</strong> = <em>tele</em> (distance) + <em>communication</em> (exchange of info)</li>\n<li><strong>Automation</strong> = <em>auto</em> (self) + <em>-tion</em> (process)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Building Blocks of Professional Vocabulary</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Building Block</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Roots</td>\n<td>Core meaning-unit, often from Latin/Greek</td>\n<td><em>\"struct\"</em> (to build) → construct, structure, instruct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prefixes</td>\n<td>Added before a root to change meaning</td>\n<td><em>\"re-\"</em> (again) → rebuild, restart, retest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suffixes</td>\n<td>Added after a root to change word form (noun/verb/adjective)</td>\n<td><em>\"-tion\"</em> (makes a noun) → construction, instruction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Word Families</td>\n<td>Groups of related words sharing a root</td>\n<td>analyze (verb), analysis (noun), analytical (adjective), analytically (adverb)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>How Vocabulary Grows Over a Career</strong> <em>(detailed notes)</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reading widely — technical papers, professional emails, news, reports.</li>\n<li>Active noticing — pausing on unfamiliar or precise words instead of skipping them.</li>\n<li>Contextual learning — understanding a word through the sentence it appears in, not just a dictionary definition.</li>\n<li>Repetition and usage — using a new word in your own writing/speech multiple times to make it stick.</li>\n<li>Field-specific exposure — e.g. engineers build vocabulary like \"latency,\" \"throughput,\" \"tolerance,\" \"iteration.\"</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Building vocabulary is like building a toolbox. A beginner has a hammer for everything. An expert has the exact right tool (word) for each specific job (meaning).</p>\n<h4>Precision in Word Choice</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denotation vs. Connotation</strong></li>\n<li>Denotation = literal/dictionary meaning</li>\n<li>Connotation = emotional/cultural feeling a word carries</li>\n<li>Example: <em>\"self-assured\"</em> (positive connotation) vs. <em>\"arrogant\"</em> (negative connotation) — describe similar traits, different feel.</li>\n<li><strong>Commonly Confused Words</strong> — use accurately: <em>effect</em> (noun, result) vs. <em>affect</em> (verb, to influence); <em>accepted</em> (agreed to) vs. <em>excepted</em> (excluded).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Why Precision Matters &amp; Vague vs. Precise Examples</strong>\nVague word choice can create ambiguity in specifications, reports, and instructions — leading to costly misunderstandings.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vague</th>\n<th>Precise</th>\n<th>Why It's Better</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"The system is slow.\"</td>\n<td>\"The system's response time exceeds 3 seconds under peak load.\"</td>\n<td>Gives a measurable, actionable detail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"There was a problem.\"</td>\n<td>\"The database connection timed out after 30 seconds.\"</td>\n<td>Identifies the specific issue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Make it better.\"</td>\n<td>\"Optimize the algorithm to reduce time complexity from O(n²) to O(n log n).\"</td>\n<td>Specifies exactly what \"better\" means.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"A lot of users complained.\"</td>\n<td>\"42% of surveyed users reported login failures.\"</td>\n<td>Quantifies the claim.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Near-Synonyms Are Not Identical</strong> <em>(expands on denotation/connotation above)</em>\n| Word Group | Subtle Difference |\n|---|---|\n| <em>error</em> vs. <em>bug</em> vs. <em>defect</em> vs. <em>fault</em> | \"Error\" = general mistake; \"bug\" = informal coding flaw; \"defect\" = formal, QA/testing; \"fault\" = hardware/systems context |\n| <em>fast</em> vs. <em>rapid</em> vs. <em>swift</em> vs. <em>hasty</em> | \"Hasty\" implies carelessness; \"swift\" implies skillful speed |\n| <em>change</em> vs. <em>modify</em> vs. <em>revise</em> vs. <em>overhaul</em> | \"Overhaul\" implies a complete, major change; \"modify\" implies a smaller adjustment |</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> When unsure which word to use, ask: <em>\"What is the exact idea I want to express, and does this word match it precisely?\"</em></p>\n<h4>Professional/Corporate Vocabulary <em>(definitions — likely direct exam question)</em></h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakeholder</td>\n<td>A person/group with an interest or concern in a project</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deadline</td>\n<td>The latest time/date something should be completed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deliverable</td>\n<td>A thing to be provided, esp. as an output of a project</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milestone</td>\n<td>A significant stage or event in a project's development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accountability</td>\n<td>Being accountable; responsibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Professional Vocabulary Categories</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Words for Actions/Requests</strong>\n| Casual | Professional |\n|---|---|\n| \"tell me\" | \"kindly inform me\" / \"please advise\" |\n| \"fix it\" | \"resolve the issue\" |\n| \"check it\" | \"review\" / \"verify\" |\n| \"look into\" | \"investigate\" |</p>\n<p><strong>B. Words for Meetings/Collaboration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>align</em> (get agreement) — \"Let's align on the timeline.\"</li>\n<li><em>circle back</em> (return to a topic later) — \"We'll circle back to this next week.\"</li>\n<li><em>touch base</em> (make brief contact) — \"Let's touch base tomorrow.\"</li>\n<li><em>escalate</em> (raise an issue to higher authority) — \"We need to escalate this bug.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Words for Status/Progress</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>in progress</em>, <em>pending</em>, <em>on track</em>, <em>behind schedule</em>, <em>finalized</em>, <em>pending approval</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Caution:</strong> Professional vocabulary should still be <strong>clear</strong>, not just impressive-sounding. Using professional words correctly is good; using them to sound fancy while confusing the reader defeats the purpose (see Session 7 on corporate-speak).</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2", "title": "SESSION 6 — Technical vs. General Vocabulary", "html": "<p><strong>Aim:</strong> Distinguish technical vs. general vocabulary; use hedging/softening language appropriately.</p>\n<h4>Technical vs. General Vocabulary</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Technical Vocabulary</th>\n<th>General Vocabulary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>Specialized words for specific fields</td>\n<td>Common, everyday words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td><em>Algorithm</em>, <em>Myocardial Infarction</em></td>\n<td><em>Step-by-step instructions</em>, <em>Heart attack</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Benefit</td>\n<td>Precision, accuracy, avoids ambiguity</td>\n<td>Connection, clarity, easy to understand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Used for</td>\n<td>Experts, research papers</td>\n<td>General audiences, everyday talk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Be precise when you must; be clear so others can understand. Explaining technical ideas to non-technical audiences = replace jargon with familiar words, examples, analogies.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] The Distinction, in Detail</strong>\n| Type | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| General Vocabulary | Words used in everyday, non-specialized communication | \"increase,\" \"problem,\" \"connect\" |\n| Technical Vocabulary | Words with specific meaning within a field, often unfamiliar to outsiders | \"throughput,\" \"latency,\" \"compiler,\" \"API,\" \"algorithm\" |</p>\n<p><strong>Same word, different meaning in different fields:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>\"Bug\"</em> (general) = insect; <em>\"bug\"</em> (software) = a flaw in code.</li>\n<li><em>\"Driver\"</em> (general) = person who drives; <em>\"driver\"</em> (computer) = software that controls hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Building Technical Vocabulary:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Learn terms <strong>in context</strong> (from documentation, textbooks, real code/specs) rather than memorizing isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Understand the concept behind the term, not just the label.</li>\n<li>Practice using the term in a sentence related to your own project/work.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Explaining Technical Ideas to Non-Technical Audiences</strong> <em>(expands the key takeaway above)</em>\nA hallmark of a truly skilled engineer is the ability to \"translate\" technical language into something simple.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Use Analogies</strong> — <em>\"A firewall is like a security guard that checks everyone entering a building before letting them in.\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Jargon or Define It Immediately</strong> — ❌ \"We need to reduce the API's latency.\" → ✅ \"We need to make the system respond faster when other software requests information from it (this delay is called 'latency').\"</li>\n<li><strong>Focus on Impact, Not Mechanism</strong> — ❌ \"We refactored the backend to use a microservices architecture.\" → ✅ \"We restructured the system so it will be more reliable and easier to update in the future.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Use the \"Layered Explanation\" Approach</strong> — start with a one-sentence simple summary, add detail only if asked.</li>\n<li>Layer 1 (Simple): \"The app crashed because it ran out of memory.\"</li>\n<li>Layer 2 (More detail): \"Specifically, a process kept allocating memory without releasing it — this is called a memory leak.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid Acronyms Without Explanation</strong> — always expand on first use: \"Application Programming Interface (API).\"</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Collocations vs. Phrasal Verbs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Collocations:</strong> words that naturally go together → <em>make a decision</em>, <em>heavy rain</em>, <em>conduct research</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Phrasal Verbs:</strong> verb + preposition/adverb = new meaning → <em>carry out</em>, <em>set up</em>, <em>turn off</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Collocations and Natural Usage</strong>\nA <strong>collocation</strong> is a combination of words that fluent speakers naturally use together — other combinations may be grammatically correct but sound \"unnatural.\"</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Natural Collocation</th>\n<th>Unnatural (Grammatically Possible but Wrong)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"make a decision\"</td>\n<td>~~\"do a decision\"~~</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"conduct research\"</td>\n<td>~~\"make research\"~~</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"raise a concern\"</td>\n<td>~~\"lift a concern\"~~</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"strong coffee\"</td>\n<td>~~\"powerful coffee\"~~</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"heavy rain\"</td>\n<td>~~\"strong rain\"~~</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"commit an error\"</td>\n<td>~~\"make an error\"~~ (note: \"make a mistake\" IS correct — collocations are word-specific!)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Common Engineering/Technical Collocations:</strong> \"run a test/program/simulation,\" \"meet a deadline/requirements/specifications,\" \"conduct an experiment/analysis,\" \"resolve an issue\" / \"address a concern\" / \"fix a bug.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why they matter:</strong> Using the wrong collocation isn't a grammar error, but it signals non-native or unnatural phrasing to a fluent reader — reducing the professionalism of your writing.</p>\n<p><strong>How to learn them:</strong> Read authentic professional/technical texts and notice word pairings; use a collocation dictionary or corpus tool when unsure; when learning a new verb, learn which nouns typically pair with it.</p>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Idioms, Phrasal Verbs, and Professional Expressions</strong>\n| Term | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Idiom | A phrase whose meaning isn't obvious from its individual words | \"hit the ground running\" = start something with full energy immediately |\n| Phrasal Verb | A verb + preposition/particle combination with a distinct meaning | \"look into\" = investigate; \"put off\" = postpone |\n| Professional Expression | Common set phrases used in workplace contexts | \"moving forward,\" \"on the same page,\" \"bring to the table\" |</p>\n<p><strong>Common Professional Phrasal Verbs:</strong> set up (establish/configure), roll out (launch/release), follow up (check on progress), carry out (perform/execute), back up (support / make a copy).</p>\n<p><strong>The risk of misusing idioms:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Overuse sounds unnatural or try-hard, especially from a learner.</li>\n<li>Idioms can be misunderstood by international/non-native audiences — defeats the purpose of clear communication (ties to Module 1's \"intelligibility\" concept).</li>\n<li>Some idioms are too informal for formal writing: ❌ \"We need to nip this bug in the bud.\" → ✅ \"We need to address this bug immediately, before it affects other components.\"</li>\n<li>Literal translation errors — idioms don't translate word-for-word across languages.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use idioms/phrasal verbs only when widely understood, appropriate for the register, and used naturally (not forced in for effect).</p>\n<h4>Tone-Carrying Words</h4>\n<p>Reveal attitude/certainty; make language polite, respectful, persuasive (<em>please, appreciate, confidently</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Same Meaning, Different Tone</strong>\n| Neutral | Positive Tone | Negative Tone |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"changed\" | \"improved,\" \"refined\" | \"altered carelessly,\" \"tampered with\" |\n| \"said\" | \"explained,\" \"clarified\" | \"claimed,\" \"insisted\" |\n| \"different\" | \"unique,\" \"innovative\" | \"unusual,\" \"inconsistent\" |\n| \"asked\" | \"requested,\" \"inquired\" | \"demanded,\" \"interrogated\" |</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters for engineers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In bug reports/reviews: \"The developer <em>failed</em> to handle edge cases\" (harsh) vs. \"The code does not currently handle edge cases\" (neutral, professional).</li>\n<li>In emails: \"You <em>need</em> to send this today\" (demanding) vs. \"Could you please send this today?\" (polite, tone-appropriate).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Hedging &amp; Softening Language</h4>\n<p>Cautious/qualifying words that soften absolute claims.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absolute: <em>Night shifts cause sleep disorders.</em></li>\n<li>Hedged: <em>Night shifts might/may/possibly cause sleep disorders.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes]</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"This <em>might</em> cause performance issues\" (softer) vs. \"This <em>will</em> cause performance issues\" (absolute — only use if certain)</li>\n<li>\"It <em>appears</em> that...\" / \"This <em>suggests</em>...\" / \"It <em>seems</em> likely that...\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Assertive vs. Tentative Language</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n<th>Used for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Assertive</td>\n<td>Confidence/certainty</td>\n<td>Facts, instructions, decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tentative</td>\n<td>Caution/uncertainty</td>\n<td>Opinions, suggestions, diplomacy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3", "title": "SESSION 7 — Applying Vocabulary Precision", "html": "<p><strong>Aim:</strong> Avoid vague corporate language; use AI tools responsibly to grow vocabulary.</p>\n<h4>Clichés vs. Buzzwords vs. Corporate-Speak</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cliché</td>\n<td>Overused expression, lost impact</td>\n<td><em>Think outside the box</em>, <em>At the end of the day</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buzzword</td>\n<td>Trendy word used to sound impressive</td>\n<td><em>Innovation, Disruption, Scalable, Agile</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate-speak</td>\n<td>Long, complex, buzzword-filled sentences hiding the real message</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Bad → Good rewrite (classic exam example):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ <em>\"We are leveraging synergies to optimize impactful outcomes.\"</em></li>\n<li>✅ <em>\"We are working together to improve results.\"</em></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use plain English, be specific, shorten sentences, focus on meaning over style.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Why Avoid Them &amp; More Rewrite Examples</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>They often replace real substance with vague-sounding filler.</li>\n<li>Overuse makes writing feel insincere or lazy.</li>\n<li>They can obscure the actual meaning — especially dangerous in technical/engineering communication where precision matters.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Corporate-Speak (Vague)</th>\n<th>With Substance (Clear)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"We need to leverage synergies to move the needle.\"</td>\n<td>\"We need our teams to collaborate so we can improve performance.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Let's touch base and circle back to ideate.\"</td>\n<td>\"Let's meet briefly to discuss ideas.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"This is a paradigm shift in our approach.\"</td>\n<td>\"This is a significant change in how we approach the problem.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"We're pivoting to a more agile framework.\"</td>\n<td>\"We're changing our process to work in shorter, more flexible cycles.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>How to write with substance:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Be specific — replace vague claims with concrete facts, numbers, or examples.</li>\n<li>Avoid stacking buzzwords — one vague term is bad; three in a row is worse.</li>\n<li>Ask: <em>\"If I removed this phrase, would the reader lose any actual information?\"</em> If not, cut it.</li>\n<li>Prefer plain, direct verbs over inflated jargon (\"use\" instead of \"utilize,\" \"help\" instead of \"facilitate\").</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>AI for Vocabulary Growth — 4 uses</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Generate contextual examples (how a word is used in different scenarios)</li>\n<li>Check nuance (e.g., <em>\"stubborn\"</em> vs. <em>\"determined\"</em>)</li>\n<li>Verify collocations (which words naturally pair)</li>\n<li>Build a personal word bank (track words, meanings, examples, collocations)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Best Practices:</strong> Don't just copy AI-generated sentences. Always verify context, tone, and collocations. Use good prompts to get useful examples.</p>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Effective Strategies for Using AI</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Context-based word learning</strong> — <em>\"Give me 5 sentences using the word 'mitigate' in an engineering context.\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Precision practice</strong> — <em>\"What's the difference between 'error,' 'bug,' 'defect,' and 'fault'? Give examples of when to use each.\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Collocation checking</strong> — <em>\"Is 'raise an issue' or 'lift an issue' the correct collocation?\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Register/tone conversion practice</strong> — <em>\"Rewrite this in a more formal tone\" / \"Make this sound more concise and professional.\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Buzzword/cliché detection</strong> — <em>\"Identify any clichés or corporate buzzwords in this paragraph and suggest clearer alternatives.\"</em></li>\n<li><strong>Building a personal vocabulary log</strong> — after each AI interaction, note 2–3 new words/expressions with example sentences, and use them within the week (active recall beats passive reading).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Cautions:</strong> Don't just copy AI's suggested word without understanding it — check the meaning and try using it independently. AI-generated vocabulary can sometimes be overly formal or slightly unnatural — cross-check with real-world usage when possible. The goal is internalizing vocabulary, not outsourcing word choice to AI permanently.</p>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick Revision Summary (session-tagged, last-minute recall)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S5:</strong> Root/Prefix/Suffix + examples (telecommunication, automation) + word families. Denotation vs. connotation. Commonly confused words + near-synonyms (error/bug/defect/fault). Corporate vocabulary terms (stakeholder, deadline, deliverable, milestone, accountability) + expanded action/meeting/status word categories.</li>\n<li><strong>S6:</strong> Technical vs. General vocabulary (table) + same-word-different-field examples (bug, driver). Explaining technical ideas to non-technical audiences (analogies, avoid jargon, focus on impact, layered explanation). Collocations vs. phrasal verbs + idioms and their risks. Tone-carrying words. Hedging/softening. Assertive vs. tentative language.</li>\n<li><strong>S7:</strong> Clichés vs. buzzwords vs. corporate-speak (with bad→good rewrite + extra rewrite examples). 4(+6) ways to use AI for vocabulary growth + best practices/cautions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Likely exam angles:</strong> define root/prefix/suffix + give own example; denotation vs. connotation with a pair of words; match corporate-vocabulary terms to definitions; rewrite a \"corporate-speak\" sentence in plain English; identify assertive vs. tentative in given sentences; hedge a given absolute statement; distinguish near-synonyms (error/bug/defect/fault); identify a wrong collocation and correct it.</p>"}], "practice": [], "file": "Semester_1/Language-Skills-Notes/Module2-Notes.md"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Foundations of Effective Professional Writing", "tagline": "Language Skills for Engineers · module 3", "minutes": 15, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Overview", "html": "<h3>Merged Notes (Session-wise notes + Detailed notes combined by topic)</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Each heading is tagged with the <strong>Session</strong> it belongs to from the session-wise notes. Content that only appears in the detailed notes is folded into the session it expands on, marked <strong>[Expanded — detailed notes]</strong>. One topic (Tone in Writing) appears only in the detailed notes with no session tag — it's marked <strong>[Extra — not tied to a session]</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1", "title": "SESSION 8 — Principles, Practices & Application", "html": "<h4>The 7 Cs of Effective Professional Writing <em>(high-priority list — exam favorite)</em></h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>C</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarity</td>\n<td>Message understood on first read; no ambiguity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concise</td>\n<td>Every word earns its place; cut excess without losing meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct</td>\n<td>Accurate grammar, spelling, punctuation, facts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complete</td>\n<td>Includes all info the reader needs to act/decide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Courteous</td>\n<td>Respectful, polite, positive — no sarcasm/aggression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Considerate</td>\n<td>Focuses on reader's needs, knowledge, perspective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concrete</td>\n<td>Uses specific facts, data, vivid examples — not vague generalities</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] The Core Idea</strong>\nEffective professional writing isn't about sounding intelligent — it's about <em>transferring meaning from your mind to the reader's mind as accurately and efficiently as possible.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Detailed notes' 8-quality version</strong> (adds <strong>Coherence</strong> as its own quality, which the session notes cover separately in the Coherence vs. Cohesion topic below):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quality</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarity</td>\n<td>The reader understands the message on first reading, without confusion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correctness</td>\n<td>Free from grammar, spelling, and factual errors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conciseness</td>\n<td>Says what's needed without unnecessary words (builds on Module 2's concision).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Completeness</td>\n<td>Includes all necessary information — no missing steps or context.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coherence</td>\n<td>Ideas flow logically from one to the next.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Courtesy</td>\n<td>Respectful, professional tone appropriate to the audience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concreteness</td>\n<td>Specific facts and details rather than vague generalizations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consideration</td>\n<td>Written with the reader's needs, knowledge level, and time constraints in mind.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Good professional writing is like a well-engineered bridge — every part (word, sentence, paragraph) exists for a reason, nothing is wasted, and it reliably gets the reader from point A (question) to point B (understanding).</p>\n<h4>Audience &amp; Purpose Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Audience Analysis:</strong> who they are (expert/non-expert), what they know, what they need, their attitude, the context.</li>\n<li><strong>Purpose Analysis:</strong> every document has one primary purpose — <strong>Inform, Persuade, Request, Instruct, or Record</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Why This Comes First</strong>\nBefore writing a single word, ask: (1) <em>Who am I writing for?</em> (Audience) and (2) <em>Why am I writing this?</em> (Purpose). Skipping this step is the #1 cause of ineffective professional writing.</p>\n<p><strong>Analyzing Your Audience — questions to ask:</strong>\n| Ask Yourself | Why It Matters |\n|---|---|\n| What is their technical background? | Determines how much jargon/explanation is needed (links to Module 2, Section 5). |\n| What is their relationship to me? (peer, manager, client) | Determines formality/register (links to Module 1, Section 6). |\n| What do they already know? | Avoids repeating unnecessary info or skipping critical context. |\n| What do they need to do after reading? | Shapes how action items are presented. |\n| How much time do they have? | Determines length and structure (busy executives need summaries first). |</p>\n<p><strong>Analyzing Your Purpose</strong> — common purposes: to inform, to persuade, to request, to instruct, to document (record).\n<strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> One piece of writing should have <strong>one primary purpose</strong> — mixing purposes in one unstructured document confuses readers.</p>\n<h4>Coherence vs. Cohesion <em>(commonly confused pair — likely direct question)</em></h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cohesion</td>\n<td>Surface-level links — pronouns, transition words (<em>however, therefore</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coherence</td>\n<td>Deeper logical organization — one idea/paragraph, logical sequence, consistent focus, signposting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] The Distinction</strong>\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Coherence | The overall <em>logical flow</em> of ideas — does the writing make sense as a whole? |\n| Cohesion | The <em>grammatical/lexical connections</em> between sentences — do sentences link smoothly? |</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Coherence is like a well-planned road trip route (the overall journey makes sense). Cohesion is like the road signs and connecting highways that get you smoothly from one stop to the next.</p>\n<p><strong>Achieving Cohesion — Linking Devices</strong></p>\n<p><em>A. Transition Words/Phrases</em>\n| Purpose | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Adding information | \"additionally,\" \"furthermore,\" \"moreover\" |\n| Showing contrast | \"however,\" \"on the other hand,\" \"although\" |\n| Showing cause/effect | \"therefore,\" \"as a result,\" \"consequently\" |\n| Sequencing | \"first,\" \"next,\" \"subsequently,\" \"finally\" |\n| Giving examples | \"for example,\" \"for instance,\" \"such as\" |</p>\n<p><em>B. Pronoun Reference</em> — use pronouns (it, this, these, they) to refer back to something already mentioned, but keep the reference unambiguous:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>❌ \"The server crashed after the update was installed. It caused major downtime.\" (unclear: crash or update?)</li>\n<li>✅ \"The server crashed after the update was installed. This crash caused major downtime.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>C. Repetition of Key Terms</em> — repeating an important term (rather than switching to a synonym) keeps technical writing precise.</p>\n<p><strong>Achieving Coherence:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Logical ordering (chronological, priority-based, cause-effect).</li>\n<li>Consistent focus — don't jump between unrelated topics without warning.</li>\n<li>Clear relationships — the reader should understand <em>why</em> one sentence follows another.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2", "title": "SESSION 9 — Structuring Writing & Plain Language", "html": "<h4>Paragraph Mechanics — The Three Pillars</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Topic Sentence</strong> — introduces the core focus immediately</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Details</strong> — explains, proves, or illustrates the claim</li>\n<li><strong>Transition Sentence</strong> — links cleanly to the next concept</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Worked Example</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The current login system has a critical security flaw. [topic sentence] It stores passwords without encryption, making them vulnerable if the database is breached. Additionally, it lacks rate-limiting, allowing brute-force attacks. [supporting details] These issues must be addressed before the next release. [transition/closing]\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>BLUF Approach (Bottom Line Up Front)</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Style</th>\n<th>Order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional</td>\n<td>Background → Details → Conclusion (buried at bottom)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BLUF (Inverted Pyramid)</td>\n<td>Bottom Line (Action/Decision) → Supporting Details → Background</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>BLUF gets to the point in under 3 seconds — used heavily in memos.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Other Structural Patterns</strong>\nBLUF corresponds to the <strong>Direct (Front-Loaded) Structure</strong> below; the detailed notes add three more patterns for different situations:</p>\n<p><strong>A. Direct (Front-Loaded) Structure</strong> — most common; used when audience is busy or news is neutral/positive.\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. Main point / conclusion / request (stated first)\n2. Supporting details / explanation\n3. Next steps / closing\n</code></pre></p>\n<p><strong>B. Indirect Structure</strong> — used for bad news or sensitive messages, to prepare the reader first.\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. Context / buffer statement\n2. Explanation / reasoning\n3. Main point (bad news, softened)\n4. Constructive close / alternative\n</code></pre></p>\n<p><strong>C. Problem-Solution Structure</strong> — common in technical proposals and reports.\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. Problem statement\n2. Analysis / causes\n3. Proposed solution(s)\n4. Recommendation\n</code></pre></p>\n<p><strong>D. Chronological Structure</strong> — used for process descriptions, incident reports, project timelines.\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. What happened first\n2. What happened next\n3. What happened after that\n</code></pre></p>\n<h4>Plain Language Techniques</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Utilize</td>\n<td>Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facilitate</td>\n<td>Help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In the event that</td>\n<td>If</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active vs. Passive Voice:</strong> Use Active Voice (<strong>Actor → Action → Recipient</strong>) to drive accountability and readability. Avoid the \"Passive Trap.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Principles of Plain Language</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Use common words over complex ones when precision allows (utilize→use, facilitate→help/enable, in the event that→if).</li>\n<li>Prefer active voice: ❌ \"The report was reviewed by the manager.\" → ✅ \"The manager reviewed the report.\"</li>\n<li>Use short sentences and paragraphs — aim for 15–20 words/sentence average; break paragraphs into 3–5 sentences.</li>\n<li>Use lists and headings for complex, multi-step or multi-item information.</li>\n<li>Define necessary technical terms — plain language means <em>explaining</em> jargon when needed, not avoiding it entirely (links to Module 2, Section 5).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Readability</strong> — how easy a text is to read/understand, often measured by formulas (e.g., Flesch-Kincaid) based on sentence length and word complexity.\n<strong>Self-check:</strong> Could a colleague from a different team understand this in one read? Any sentence you'd need to read twice?</p>\n<h4>Designing for Scannability (F-Pattern)</h4>\n<p>Use descriptive headings, bulleted/ordered lists, and white space to reduce cognitive overload and let readers scan quickly.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3", "title": "SESSION 10 — Common Short Professional Documents", "html": "<h4>The Writing Process — 5 Steps <em>(sequence question likely)</em></h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Planning</strong> — understand purpose, audience; gather information</li>\n<li><strong>Outlining</strong> — organize ideas into a logical structure (roadmap)</li>\n<li><strong>Drafting</strong> — get thoughts on paper without worrying about perfection</li>\n<li><strong>Revising</strong> — big picture: structure, logic, clarity</li>\n<li><strong>Editing</strong> — polish mechanics: grammar, spelling, formatting</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Detailed Notes' 5-Stage Version</strong>\n⚠️ Note: the detailed notes give a slightly different 5th step — <strong>Proofreading</strong> instead of ending at Editing, and don't list \"Outlining\" as its own stage. Know both versions for exam safety.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>What Happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Planning (Prewriting)</td>\n<td>Define audience, purpose, and key message. Gather information/facts needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Drafting</td>\n<td>Write a first version focused on getting ideas down — not perfection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Revising</td>\n<td>Review for structure, clarity, coherence, and completeness — reorganize if needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Editing</td>\n<td>Fine-tune grammar, word choice, tone, and sentence-level accuracy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Proofreading</td>\n<td>Final check for typos, punctuation, and formatting errors (see Module 1, Section 9).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Why separate stages matter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drafting and editing are different mental tasks — trying to write perfectly on the first attempt slows you down and often produces worse writing than drafting freely, then revising.</li>\n<li>Revising ≠ Proofreading. Revising = <em>big-picture</em> changes (structure, missing content); proofreading = <em>small-picture</em> fixes (typos, punctuation). Doing them in the wrong order wastes effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Tip:</strong> Step away for a few minutes before revising — creates mental distance to spot issues (same idea as Module 1's proofreading advice).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Tone: The \"You-Attitude\"</h4>\n<p>Reader-centric style; shifts focus from \"I/we\" to \"you/your.\" Emphasizes what the reader gains; protects their time; solves their problems.</p>\n<h4>Short Documents</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Document</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>Key Feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Notes</td>\n<td>Objective record of meetings/reviews</td>\n<td>Clear past tense + Action Matrix (Task | Person | Deadline)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memos</td>\n<td>Internal updates or policy changes</td>\n<td>BLUF approach + standard headers (TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short Reports</td>\n<td>Data-driven documents for decisions</td>\n<td>Technical layout: Intro → Findings → Conclusion → Recommendations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Fuller Document Overview Table</strong>\n| Document Type | Purpose | Typical Structure |\n|---|---|---|\n| Email | Quick communication, requests, updates | Subject line → greeting → purpose (first line) → details → action/closing |\n| Memo | Internal communication within an organization | Header (To/From/Date/Subject) → purpose → details → action |\n| Meeting Minutes | Record of what was discussed/decided | Attendees → agenda items → decisions → action items with owners |\n| Status Report | Update on project progress | Summary → completed work → in-progress work → blockers → next steps |\n| Proposal | Suggest a course of action | Problem → proposed solution → benefits → cost/resources → call to action |\n| Bug/Incident Report | Document a technical issue | Summary → steps to reproduce → expected vs. actual result → impact → suggested fix |</p>\n<p><strong>Email best practices:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Clear, specific subject line: ❌ \"Update\" → ✅ \"Server Downtime Update – Action Needed by Friday\"</li>\n<li>State purpose in the first line or two — don't bury the request.</li>\n<li>Use short paragraphs and bullet points for scannability.</li>\n<li>End with a clear call to action — what's needed, by when.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Meeting Minutes best practices:</strong> Record decisions, not just discussion. Always assign owners and deadlines to action items. Keep language factual and neutral.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4", "title": "SESSION 11 — Professional Writing & Drafting (Integrity & AI)", "html": "<h4>Handling Sources <em>(definitions — likely match/define question)</em></h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Summarising</td>\n<td>Condensing the main idea in your own words (much shorter)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paraphrasing</td>\n<td>Restating specific ideas in your own words and sentence structure (similar length). Changing just a few words = <strong>plagiarism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quoting</td>\n<td>Using exact words from the source, in quotation marks, copied exactly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attributing</td>\n<td>Always give credit to the original author, e.g., <em>Smith (2020) argues that...</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Summarising — Rules for Accuracy</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Capture the main idea and key supporting points — not every detail.</li>\n<li>Use your own sentence structure and wording — not the original phrasing.</li>\n<li>Do not add your own opinions or interpretations.</li>\n<li>Keep it proportionally shorter than the original.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Paraphrasing — Rules for Accuracy</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Change both the wording AND the sentence structure — simply swapping a few synonyms is not true paraphrasing and can still count as plagiarism.</li>\n<li>Preserve the original meaning exactly — don't distort or oversimplify to the point of changing the message.</li>\n<li>Always attribute the source, even when paraphrasing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Original: \"The rapid adoption of cloud computing has fundamentally transformed how organizations manage data infrastructure.\"\n❌ Poor paraphrase (just synonym-swapped): \"The fast adoption of cloud computing has basically changed how organizations handle data infrastructure.\"\n✅ Good paraphrase (restructured): \"Organizations now manage their data infrastructure very differently, largely because cloud computing has been adopted so quickly.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Quoting and Attribution:</strong> Use a direct quote when exact wording matters (legal clause, precise technical definition, notable statement) or paraphrasing would lose nuance.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Correct attribution format: According to [Author/Source, Year], \"[exact quoted text].\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Plagiarism — presenting someone else's words/ideas as your own — is a serious integrity violation; in engineering this applies to copying documentation, code comments, specifications, or reports without credit. <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> if an idea, structure, or specific phrase isn't originally yours, credit the source.</p>\n<h4>Using AI as a Drafting &amp; Revision Partner</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Appropriate</th>\n<th>Inappropriate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Brainstorming, outlining, grammar checks, structural feedback</td>\n<td>Submitting unchanged AI work, faking references, replacing your own thinking</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Golden Rule:</strong> AI should assist learning, not replace it. Always review, edit, and fact-check AI output.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>[Expanded — detailed notes] Effective AI Uses by Writing Stage</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Planning</strong> — brainstorm structure (\"What sections should a status report for a delayed project typically include?\"); clarify audience needs (\"How should I adjust this explanation for a non-technical client?\").</li>\n<li><strong>Drafting</strong> — generate a rough first draft from bullet points to overcome \"blank page\" hesitation; treat it as a starting point, not a final product.</li>\n<li><strong>Revising</strong> — ask AI to check structure/coherence (\"Does this report flow logically? Are there any gaps?\") and flag weak transitions.</li>\n<li><strong>Editing</strong> — check tone-appropriateness (\"Is this email too casual for a client?\"); simplify jargon-heavy sentences for non-technical readers (Module 2, Section 5).</li>\n<li><strong>Proofreading</strong> — use AI for a final grammar/punctuation pass, but still do a manual read-through since AI can miss context-specific errors or alter meaning.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Best practices:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Always verify facts AI includes or restates — it can generate plausible-sounding but incorrect information.</li>\n<li>Preserve your own voice — over-reliance on AI phrasing makes writing sound generic.</li>\n<li>Use AI to learn, not just to produce — ask it to explain <em>why</em> it made a suggested change.</li>\n<li>Never submit AI-generated summaries/paraphrases of sources without checking them against the original for accuracy and proper attribution — AI can distort meaning or omit necessary citation.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5", "title": "[Extra — not tied to a session] Tone in Writing (Whole-Document Tone)", "html": "<p><em>Builds on Module 2, Section 8 — Tone-Carrying Words. That section covered how individual <strong>words</strong> carry tone; this section covers how tone is <strong>sustained across an entire document</strong> to shape the reader's overall impression.</em></p>\n<h4>Common Professional Tones</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tone</th>\n<th>When Used</th>\n<th>Example Phrase</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal/Objective</td>\n<td>Reports, technical documentation</td>\n<td>\"The analysis indicates a 15% performance improvement.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polite/Courteous</td>\n<td>Requests, client communication</td>\n<td>\"We would appreciate your feedback at your earliest convenience.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct/Assertive</td>\n<td>Urgent issues, clear instructions</td>\n<td>\"This issue must be resolved before deployment.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Empathetic</td>\n<td>Apologies, sensitive communication</td>\n<td>\"We understand this delay may have caused inconvenience.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Encouraging</td>\n<td>Feedback, team communication</td>\n<td>\"Great progress on this feature — let's refine the edge cases next.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Maintaining Consistent Tone</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid shifting tone abruptly within one document (e.g., starting formal, then becoming casual).</li>\n<li>Match tone to the relationship and context, not personal preference.</li>\n<li>Stay professional even when delivering criticism — focus on the issue, not the person:</li>\n<li>❌ \"You made a careless mistake in the report.\"</li>\n<li>✅ \"There's an inconsistency in the report's figures that needs correction.\"</li>\n</ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick Revision Summary (session-tagged, last-minute recall)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S8:</strong> 7 Cs (Clarity, Concise, Correct, Complete, Courteous, Considerate, Concrete) + detailed notes add Coherence as an 8th quality. Audience &amp; Purpose analysis (5 purposes: Inform/Persuade/Request/Instruct/Record) + audience-analysis question checklist. Coherence vs. Cohesion + linking devices (transitions, pronoun reference, repetition).</li>\n<li><strong>S9:</strong> 3 paragraph pillars (Topic/Support/Transition). BLUF vs. Traditional structure + 3 more structural patterns (Indirect, Problem-Solution, Chronological). Plain language swaps + 5 plain-language principles + readability. Active voice (Actor→Action→Recipient). F-Pattern scannability.</li>\n<li><strong>S10:</strong> 5-step writing process (Plan→Outline→Draft→Revise→Edit) — note the detailed notes' alternate 5-stage version ends in Proofreading instead of Outlining. You-Attitude. Notes vs. Memos vs. Short Reports (+ expanded table: Email, Minutes, Status Report, Proposal, Bug/Incident Report) + best practices.</li>\n<li><strong>S11:</strong> Summarising vs. Paraphrasing vs. Quoting vs. Attributing (+ worked paraphrase example; near-verbatim paraphrase = plagiarism). AI use — appropriate vs. inappropriate + Golden Rule + stage-by-stage AI uses and best practices.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra (no session):</strong> Whole-document tone — common professional tones and maintaining consistency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Likely exam angles:</strong> list/define the 7 Cs with one line each; explain BLUF vs. traditional structure (and name the other 3 structural patterns); sequence the 5 writing-process steps (watch for the Outlining vs. Proofreading discrepancy between note sets); differentiate Notes/Memo/Short Report; differentiate summarising/paraphrasing/quoting (and why near-verbatim paraphrase is plagiarism); explain \"You-Attitude\" with an example rewrite; appropriate vs. inappropriate AI use scenarios; distinguish coherence vs. cohesion with an example.</p>"}], "practice": [], "file": "Semester_1/Language-Skills-Notes/Module3-Notes.md"}];
